<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		Developer ID profile — the entitlements a PUBLIC, notarized
		release ships with. Declared in desktop/package.json
		(build.mac.entitlements), which afterPack.js reads rather than
		naming a file of its own.

		This profile deliberately does NOT contain
		com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation or
		com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory. Both
		exist only to work around a self-signed certificate's EMPTY
		TeamIdentifier, which makes dyld4 treat the app and the Electron
		Framework as having "different Team IDs" under hardened-runtime
		library validation. A real Developer ID certificate has a
		non-empty Team ID, every Mach-O in the bundle — Electron
		Framework, helpers, and every bundled Python .so and dylib — is
		signed with it, and library validation passes on its own.

		Shipping those two relaxations to the public would permanently
		disable library validation in the released binary (any dylib
		would be accepted) and grant W+X memory, for a reason that does
		not apply to that build. `allow-jit` covers what V8 actually
		needs and is kept.

		The self-signed build gets entitlements.mac.selfsigned.plist,
		selected by afterPack.js from the identity itself, so neither
		profile can be used for the wrong kind of build.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
